For generic Unicode character ranges, you can specify codepoints using the \u{} construct. The below snippet also shows how to get codepoints (numerical value of a character) in Ruby. # to get codepoints from string
>> 'fox:αλεπού'.codepoints.map { '%x' % _1 }
=> ["66", "6f", "78", "3a", "3b1", "3bb", "3b5", "3c0", "3bf", "3cd"]
# one or more codepoints can be specified inside \u{}
>> puts "\u{66 6f 78 3a 3b1 3bb 3b5 3c0 3bf 3cd}"
fox:αλεπού # character range example using \u{}
# all english lowercase letters
>> 'fox:αλεπού,eagle:αετός'.scan(/[\u{61}-\u{7a}]+/)
=> ["fox", "eagle"] info See also: codepoints , a site dedicated for Unicode characters.

